INT. FUNICULAR CAR, HALFWAY UP - NIGHT

A wood-paneled funicular car stalled on a steep incline above a sleeping Swiss lake town. Rain on the glass. One brass lamp burns.

MADAME ODILE VANTERPOOL (60s, fur collar, rings on every finger) sits very upright on the forward bench. Across from her, INSPECTOR HOLLIS CRANE (40s, rumpled trench coat, a hat he keeps turning in his hands) studies the floor. Neither speaks.

Wind shifts the car. The cable creaks.

An intercom grille crackles beside the door.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
(through the grille)
Folks, we have a fault on the lower winch. Twenty minutes, perhaps thirty. Please remain seated.

Crane glances at the grille, then at Odile, who has not looked up. She opens her handbag, takes out a small pearl-handled revolver, sets it on her knee as one might set down a glove, and closes the bag again. Crane watches this, sets his hat on the bench beside him, and slides a hand slowly into his coat pocket and out again with a cigarette case. He offers her one. She declines with two fingers. He lights his own, and the match shakes just once.

CRANE
Terrible weather.

ODILE
It does this in October.

CRANE
Your husband used to say that, I understand.

ODILE
My husband said a great many things.

The lamp flickers. Crane exhales smoke toward the ceiling.

CRANE
He was found at the bottom of this very hill. Same hour. Same rain.

ODILE
I heard.

CRANE
You didn't come to the funeral.

ODILE
I was unwell.

CRANE
Yet here you are, riding the last car of the night, to a villa you've told everyone you have not visited in a year.

She turns the revolver a quarter-inch on her knee, so the muzzle points at the door instead of at him.

ODILE
Inspector, you've been very patient. Say what you came to say.

CRANE
The conductor remembers a woman in a fur collar on the evening car. The night of.

ODILE
The conductor is mistaken.

CRANE
He was very sure. He said she tipped him. Said she wore rings on every finger.

Silence. The cable creaks. Odile lays one ringed hand flat over the revolver and does not lift it.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
(through the grille)
Fault located. Ten more minutes, folks. Thank you for your patience.

Crane stubs the cigarette out on the sole of his shoe and puts the butt in his pocket, neat as ever. Odile picks up the revolver and holds it in her lap with both hands. Crane does not reach for his hat. He does not reach for anything.

CRANE
You could shoot me. Before they get the car moving.

ODILE
I could.

CRANE
Nobody would hear it over the rain.

ODILE
No.

CRANE
Then why haven't you?

She stands. The car sways. The muzzle comes up, level, steady, and she crosses the three steps between them and puts it against his tie.

ODILE
Because you are the first person in a year who has said his name aloud to me.

She pulls the hammer back.

CRANE
Odile.

The intercom crackles. The car lurches forward and begins to climb.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
(through the grille)
And we're moving! Sorry for the delay!

The lamp swings. Her finger tightens.
